      "math_academy_id": "e-1091",
      "problem": "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac {\\sqrt{y}} {\\sqrt[3]{y}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the radicals as fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt[3]{y}}=\\frac{{y}^{1/2}}{{y}^{1/3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{1/2}}{{y}^{1/3}} & = \\\\ {y}^{1/2-1/3} & = \\\\ {y}^{3/6-2/6} & = \\\\ {y}^{1/6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we express the result as a radical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/6}=\\sqrt[6]{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
